        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherently placed and idiomatic for this class:\n\n- **Right unit.** `OwnerTelephone` already owns \"normalize to E.164 and return null if the result isn't valid E.164.\" The national-length-per-country rule is another facet of that same validity judgment, so extending this class (rather than introducing a separate validator) is the natural home. The new rule layers on top of the existing `E164.matches()` check rather than tangling with it.\n\n- **No catch-all growth.** The new logic is factored into a focused private helper `hasValidNationalLength(...)` with its own Javadoc, keeping `normalize(...)` readable (fail-fast on the general rule, then delegate). The method does one thing.\n\n- **Data vs. logic separation is clean.** Per-country lengths live in the `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` table, so adding a country is a one-line data edit, not a code change. The static initializer block is the conventional way to build an unmodifiable-intent constant map here.\n\n- **The `LinkedHashMap` choice is documented with its reason** (prefix-ordering so a longer code wins over a shorter prefix), which is exactly the kind of non-obvious intent a maintainer would want recorded.\n\n- **Javadoc kept in sync.** The class-level doc was updated to describe the new rule and the \"unrecognized code passes on the general rule alone\" behavior, matching the implementation \u2014 no stale contract left behind.\n\nNothing is duplicated, and a maintainer of this codebase would find the shape natural."
